#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# coordinate_bounds_array

#% text
# `coordinate_bounds_array([[a1, b1], [a2, b2], ...])` gives the array of the grid points with unit steps in the box; `coordinate_bounds_array(bounds, d)` uses steps `d`, `(bounds, d, offset)` shifts the points, `(bounds, d, offset, pad)` widens the box first.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A step is a number or `into(n)` (`n` equal parts), one for all coordinates or a list.

#% item
# The offset is `center` (half a step), a number, `scaled(s)` (`s` steps) or a list of them.

#% item
# The points of each coordinate are `a + offset + k d` up to `b`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
coordinate_bounds_array([[3, 5], [-1, 1]])
#% output
#= [[[3, -1], [3, 0], [3, 1]], [[4, -1], [4, 0], [4, 1]], [[5, -1], [5, 0], [5, 1]]]

#% cell
coordinate_bounds_array([[3, 8], [-1, 2]], 1, center)
#% output
#= [[[7/2, -1/2], [7/2, 1/2], [7/2, 3/2]], [[9/2, -1/2], [9/2, 1/2], [9/2, 3/2]], [[11/2, -1/2], [11/2, 1/2], [11/2, 3/2]], [[13/2, -1/2], [13/2, 1/2], [13/2, 3/2]], [[15/2, -1/2], [15/2, 1/2], [15/2, 3/2]]]

#% cell
coordinate_bounds_array([[0, 10]], into(5))
#% output
#= [[0], [2], [4], [6], [8], [10]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `coordinate_bounding_box_array`, `coordinate_bounds`, `outer`
